sw obsidian/
.gstack/

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MONOREPO-WIDE rules. This file guards EVERY folder here (django_inventory,
# Django_app, DSA, …). django_inventory/.gitignore only guards its own folder,
# which is why a stray dump in Django_app/ once slipped into git history.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Database dumps = DATA, not code. A .sql dump is plaintext: committing one
# publishes every row (emails, password hashes, session keys). Git history is
# append-only, so a later delete does NOT remove it — prevention is the fix.
# Backups belong in a backup location, never in a code repo.
*.sql
*.dump
*.sql.gz
db_backups/
backups/

# Dependencies — reinstallable from the lockfile, so never worth committing.
node_modules/

# ── Escape hatch ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Need to commit a genuinely-code .sql one day (a view definition, a seed
# script)? These patterns block `git add`, so force it explicitly:
#     git add -f path/to/that.sql
# Being forced to type -f is the point: it makes committing SQL a decision
# instead of an accident.
